use std
into html.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace html). It reads an HTML page into the document model with a tolerant tag scanner (no tree): headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, preformatted blocks and block quotes become blocks, and it can also return just the visible text. Every module-level name starts with `ht_` or `HT_`. All appends to the document model go through `dm::dm_add`, `dm::dm_new_table`, `dm::dm_end_table` and `dm::dm_set_meta`; block kinds are `dm::DM_HEAD`, `dm::DM_PARA`, `dm::DM_ITEM`, `dm::DM_ROW`, `dm::DM_NOTE` and `dm::DM_CODE`. `dm::dm_add` already cleans whitespace, so text only has to be roughly right.

```amni
import dm;
```

Module words: `let HT_BLOCKS` is the string " p div section article aside nav header footer main form fieldset figure figcaption dl dt dd address hr details summary center caption legend hgroup menu dir body html ", `let HT_SKIPS` is the string " script style noscript template svg canvas iframe object ", both with a leading and trailing space so a whole word can be looked up by searching for a space, the word and a space.

`ht_in(list const byte[:], word const byte[:]) bool` is true when the list contains the word as a whole word: search list for a space followed by word followed by a space (the lists above start and end with a space). An empty word is never in a list.

`ht_lower(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends s with every ASCII upper-case letter lower-cased (using `dm::dm_lower`).

`ht_find_ci(s const byte[:], pat const byte[:], from int) int` returns the index of the first place at or after from where pat occurs in s ignoring ASCII case (pat is given in lower case), or minus one. When from is below zero start at zero.

`ht_alpha(c byte) bool` is true for ASCII letters. `ht_name_byte(c byte) bool` is true for ASCII letters, digits, minus, underscore and colon.

`ht_attr(attrs const byte[:], name const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out, then looks through attrs (the text of a tag after its name, like ` href="a b" alt=x disabled`) for an attribute called name (compare ignoring ASCII case; name is given in lower case). Walk it attribute by attribute: skip white space and slashes; read the attribute name (everything up to white space, equals sign, slash or end); skip white space; when an equals sign follows, skip white space and read the value, which is either quoted with a double or single quote (up to the matching quote, the quote may hold any other byte) or unquoted (up to white space); an attribute without a value has an empty value. When the attribute name matches, append the value to out and return true. Return false when no attribute matches.

Module state: `var ht_buf byte[>..]` (the text of the block being collected, or of the table cell), `var ht_bk = 2` and `var ht_bl = 0` (the kind and level the buffer will be added as), `var ht_lst int[>..]` (stack of open lists, minus one for an unordered list, otherwise how many items an ordered list has had), `var ht_quote = 0` (open block quotes), `var ht_pre = 0` (open pre blocks), `var ht_first = false` (a pre block just started, so a newline that comes first is dropped), `var ht_tdep = 0` (table nesting depth, 0 outside tables), `var ht_incell = false`, `var ht_inrow = false`, `var ht_row byte[>..]` (the tab separated cells of the row being collected), `var ht_head = false` (inside the head element, where text is ignored), `var ht_quiet = false` (metadata is not recorded), `var ht_tags = 0` (how many tags were seen), `var ht_title = false` (a title was found).

`ht_reset()` clears `ht_buf`, `ht_lst` and `ht_row`, sets `ht_bk` to `dm::DM_PARA`, `ht_bl` to 0, and every other module variable above (including `ht_cells`) to its starting value.

`ht_kind()` sets `ht_bk` and `ht_bl` back to the default for text outside special elements: `dm::DM_NOTE` with level 0 when `ht_quote` is above zero, otherwise `dm::DM_PARA` with level 0.

`ht_flush()` ends the block being collected. When `ht_incell` is true it only appends one space to `ht_buf` (when the buffer is not empty and does not already end in a space or newline) and returns. Otherwise it calls `dm::dm_add` with `ht_bk`, `ht_bl` and `ht_buf` and clears `ht_buf`.

`ht_put(s const byte[:])` adds a run of text from the page. First do nothing when `ht_head` is true. Decode character references with `dm::dm_decode_entities` into a temporary buffer. When `ht_pre` is above zero: when `ht_first` is true and the text starts with a newline or a carriage return and newline, drop that one line break; clear `ht_first`; then append the text unchanged to `ht_buf`. Otherwise collapse white space: go through the decoded bytes, and for each space, tab, newline, carriage return or form feed append one space only when `ht_buf` is not empty and its last byte is not a space and not a newline; any other byte is appended as is.

`ht_cell_end()` does nothing when `ht_incell` is false. Otherwise it appends the tab byte (9) to `ht_row` when `var ht_cells = 0` (the number of cells already in the current row) is above zero, appends `ht_buf` to `ht_row`, adds one to `ht_cells`, clears `ht_buf`, and sets `ht_incell` to false. An empty cell still counts, so the tab separator is written even when the previous cell was empty.

`ht_row_end()` does nothing when `ht_inrow` is false. Otherwise it calls `ht_cell_end()`, then `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_ROW, 0, ht_row)`, clears `ht_row`, sets `ht_cells` to zero and `ht_inrow` to false.

`ht_open_cell()` does `ht_cell_end()`, then (when `ht_inrow` is false) sets `ht_inrow` to true, then clears `ht_buf` and sets `ht_incell` to true.

`ht_start_table()`: when `ht_tdep` is zero call `ht_flush()`, then `dm::dm_new_table()`, then set `ht_tdep` to 1; otherwise (a table inside a cell) add one to `ht_tdep` and append a space to `ht_buf`.

`ht_end_table()`: when `ht_tdep` is zero do nothing; when it is 1 call `ht_row_end()`, `dm::dm_end_table()` and set `ht_tdep` to zero, and `ht_kind()`; when above 1 subtract one and append a space to `ht_buf`.

`ht_meta(attrs const byte[:])` handles a meta tag: when `ht_quiet` is true return. Read the attribute name; when it is exactly description, author or keywords (ignoring case) read the attribute content, decode its character references with `dm::dm_decode_entities`, and call `dm::dm_set_meta(name, value)` with the name in lower case.

`ht_img(attrs const byte[:])` handles an image tag: read the attribute alt, decode character references, collapse white space like `ht_put`, and when the result is not empty append "[image: ", the text and "]" to `ht_buf` (in a pre block or not; when `ht_head` is true do nothing).

`ht_open(name const byte[:], attrs const byte[:])` handles an opening tag whose name is lower case and increments `ht_tags` first. In this order of rules, the first that applies wins: name "head" sets `ht_head` true; name "body" sets `ht_head` false; name "meta" calls `ht_meta`; name "img" calls `ht_img`; name "br" appends a newline byte to `ht_buf` when `ht_buf` is not empty or `ht_pre` is above zero; name "pre" calls `ht_flush()`, adds one to `ht_pre`, sets `ht_first` true, `ht_bk` to `dm::DM_CODE` and `ht_bl` to 0 (when `ht_incell` is true only add one to `ht_pre` and append a space); a name that is h1 to h6 calls `ht_flush()` and sets `ht_bk` to `dm::DM_HEAD` and `ht_bl` to the digit; name "blockquote" calls `ht_flush()`, adds one to `ht_quote` and calls `ht_kind()`; names "ul" and "ol" call `ht_flush()`, push onto `ht_lst` minus one for ul and zero for ol, and call `ht_kind()`; name "li": call `ht_flush()`; when `ht_incell` is true append a space and stop; otherwise set `ht_bk` to `dm::DM_ITEM` and `ht_bl` to the length of `ht_lst` (at least 1) and, when the list stack is not empty and its top is zero or more, add one to the top and append the new top number, a dot and a space to `ht_buf`; name "table" calls `ht_start_table()`; name "tr" when `ht_tdep` is 1 calls `ht_row_end()`, then `ht_flush()`, then sets `ht_inrow` true, and when `ht_tdep` is above 1 appends a space to `ht_buf`; names "td" and "th" when `ht_tdep` is 1 call `ht_flush()` only when `ht_incell` is false and then `ht_open_cell()`, and when `ht_tdep` is above 1 append a space to `ht_buf`; any name that `ht_in` finds in `HT_BLOCKS` calls `ht_flush()`. Anything else (inline tags like a, b, span) does nothing.

`ht_close(name const byte[:])` handles a closing tag whose name is lower case: name "head" sets `ht_head` false; h1 to h6 call `ht_flush()` and `ht_kind()`; "pre": when `ht_pre` is above zero subtract one, and when it is now zero call `ht_flush()` and `ht_kind()` (in a cell just append a space); "blockquote": `ht_flush()`, subtract one from `ht_quote` when above zero, `ht_kind()`; "ul" and "ol": `ht_flush()`, pop `ht_lst` when not empty, `ht_kind()`; "li": `ht_flush()` and `ht_kind()`; "td" and "th": when `ht_tdep` is 1 call `ht_cell_end()`, otherwise when above 1 append a space to `ht_buf`; "tr": when `ht_tdep` is 1 call `ht_row_end()`; "table" calls `ht_end_table()`; any name that `ht_in` finds in `HT_BLOCKS` calls `ht_flush()`.

`ht_tag_end(s const byte[:], from int) int` returns the index of the closing greater-than sign of the tag whose attribute text starts at from, skipping over double and single quoted values (a quote only opens a value when the byte before it is an equals sign, optionally followed by spaces, and then runs to the matching quote); when there is no closing sign return the length of s.

`ht_scan(s const byte[:])` walks s (UTF-8 text) from index 0 with these rules at each position i. A position that does not start with a less-than sign: take the text up to the next less-than sign (or the end) and pass it to `ht_put`. A less-than sign followed by "!--" skips to just after the next "-->" (or the end). A less-than sign followed by "![CDATA[" passes the text up to the next "]]>" to `ht_put` and goes on after it. A less-than sign followed by an exclamation mark or a question mark skips to just after the next greater-than sign. A less-than sign followed by a letter, or by a slash and a letter, is a tag: read the name (bytes accepted by `ht_name_byte`, lower-cased with `ht_lower`) and find the end of the tag with `ht_tag_end`; the attribute text is the bytes between the name and the end, without a trailing slash; a closing tag calls `ht_close`, an opening tag calls `ht_open`; then for an opening tag: when the name is "title" take the raw text up to the next closing title tag (found with `ht_find_ci` for "</title"), decode its character references with `dm::dm_decode_entities`, call `dm::dm_set_meta("title", text)` unless `ht_quiet` is true, set `ht_title` and continue after that closing tag's greater-than sign; when the name is in `HT_SKIPS` and the tag does not end with a slash, skip everything up to and including the next closing tag of the same name (found with `ht_find_ci` for "</" followed by the name; continue after its greater-than sign, or at the end when there is none). Continue after the tag's closing greater-than sign. Any other less-than sign is ordinary text: pass a single "<" to `ht_put` and move on one byte. At the end call `ht_row_end()`, and while `ht_tdep` is above zero call `ht_end_table()`, then `ht_flush()`.

`ht_charset(data const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and, looking only at the first 4096 bytes of data, finds the first occurrence of "charset" ignoring case (`ht_find_ci`), skips white space, an equals sign, white space and an optional quote after it, and appends the following name (bytes up to a quote, semicolon, space, greater-than sign, slash or end) lower-cased. Appends nothing when there is none.

`ht_decode(data const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends data as UTF-8. When data starts with a byte order mark (239 187 191, 255 254 or 254 255) call `dm::dm_to_utf8`. Otherwise get the declared charset with `ht_charset`; when it is one of windows-1252, cp1252, iso-8859-1, iso8859-1, latin1, latin-1, l1, iso-8859-15, us-ascii or ascii, append every byte of data through `dm::dm_put_cp(out, dm::dm_cp1252(byte))`; in every other case (including utf-8, missing, or a charset this module cannot decode) call `dm::dm_to_utf8`.

`ht_read_utf8(text const byte[:]) bool` reads a page that is already UTF-8: call `ht_reset()`, `ht_scan(text)`, and return true when `ht_tags` is above zero, otherwise false (no tag at all means the input is not HTML).

`ht_read(data const byte[:]) bool` converts data to UTF-8 with `ht_decode` into a temporary buffer and returns `ht_read_utf8` of it.

`ht_text_utf8(text const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends the visible text of a page that is already UTF-8 to out without changing the document model for good: remember the lengths of `dm::dm_blocks` and `dm::dm_pool` and the values of `dm::dm_table`, call `ht_reset()`, set `ht_quiet` true, call `ht_scan(text)`, then go through the blocks added after the remembered length and append to out: for a heading, paragraph, note or code block its text and two newlines; for an item two spaces per level beyond the first, then "- " unless the text starts with digits followed by a dot and a space, then the text and a newline, plus one more newline when the next block is not an item; for a row its text (cells already separated by tab bytes) and a newline, plus one more newline when the next block is not a row of the same level. Afterwards pop the new blocks off `dm::dm_blocks`, pop the added bytes off `dm::dm_pool`, restore `dm::dm_table`, and trim trailing white space from the text appended to out (never touching what out held before). Use `dm::dm_text_of` to get a block's text.

`ht_text_only(data const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` converts data to UTF-8 with `ht_decode` into a temporary buffer and calls `ht_text_utf8` on it.
